<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Belgica Secunda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 300–5th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-belgica">Belgica</a> Secunda was a late Roman province in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, formed by the division of Gallia Belgica around 300, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>. It covered much of present-day northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tournai">Tournai</a>, Cambrai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a>. The Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/childeric-i">Childeric I</a> held Roman authority in it in the later 5th century, and it became the core of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a>'s kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/belgica-secunda.jpg" alt="Belgica Secunda" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Belgica_(125_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
